   "id": "pmt/pure-core-2/ch5/q3",
   "question": "<figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:65mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/pmt/pure-core-2-ch5/q03-fig-1.png\" alt=\"Figure 1: pole O and the initial line; the curve C rises from the initial line, curving up to touch a vertical tangent line at A and then bending back to the left; the region R between the curve, the vertical tangent at A and the initial line is shaded\"></figure><p>Figure 1 shows a sketch of the curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> with equation</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"r=1+\\tan\\theta\\qquad\\qquad0\\leqslant\\theta&lt;\\frac{\\pi}{3}\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p>Figure 1 also shows the tangent to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> at the point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span>.<br>This tangent is perpendicular to the initial line.</p><ol class=\"parts\" type=\"a\"><li>Use differentiation to prove that the polar coordinates of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span> are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left(2,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></li></ol><p>The finite region <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"R\"></span>, shown shaded in Figure 1, is bounded by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span>, the tangent at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span> and the initial line.</p><ol class=\"parts\" type=\"a\" start=\"2\"><li>Use calculus to show that the exact area of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"R\"></span> is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\dfrac12(1-\\ln2)\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></li></ol>",
   "steps": [
