   "id": "pmt/pure-core-2/ch5/q5",
   "question": "<figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:80mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/pmt/pure-core-2-ch5/q05-fig-1.png\" alt=\"Figure 1: the pole O with the initial line to the right; a small curve C1 above O and a large curve C2 mostly below O, both passing through O; the region R inside C1 and outside C2, above C2, is shaded\"></figure><p>Figure 1 shows a sketch of two curves <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C_1\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C_2\"></span> with polar equations</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"C_1\\!:\\ r=(1+\\sin\\theta)\\qquad0\\leqslant\\theta&lt;2\\pi\" data-display=\"true\"></div><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"C_2\\!:\\ r=3(1-\\sin\\theta)\\qquad0\\leqslant\\theta&lt;2\\pi\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p>The region <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"R\"></span> lies inside <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C_1\"></span> and outside <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C_2\"></span> and is shown shaded in Figure 1.</p><p>Show that the area of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"R\"></span> is</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"p\\sqrt3-q\\pi\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"p\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"q\"></span> are integers to be determined.</p>",
